damentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Joe Wilson (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's 2nd District leans Republican by about seven points, and Joe Wilson has held the seat since 2001. He turned back a primary challenge in June and enters the general election with roughly $183,000 on hand after raising about $861,000. Democrat Zyon Khalifa, a 25-year-old first-time candidate who won a runoff for the nomination, has raised under $20,000 and reported about $700 in cash. A third candidate also appears on the ballot.\n\nEvery major race rater places the district in the Solid or Likely Republican column, and prediction markets give Wilson roughly a 90 percent chance of returning to Washington. No public general-election polls of the district are available, so the outlook rests on district lean, incumbency, money, and the national climate. A favorable midterm environment for Democrats is the main force that could narrow the race, but the evidence points to a comfortable Wilson win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -9.56}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0576, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0526, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0819, "margin": -10.0}]}